<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xml:lang="EN" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Neural Transplant Plast</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">NP</journal-id><journal-title>Journal of Neural Transplantation &#x00026; Plasticity</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0792-8483</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">7578438</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2565296</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1155/NP.1994.223</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Isolation of Enteric Ganglia from the Myenteric Plexus of Adult Rats</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Jaeger</surname><given-names>Christine B.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"/><xref ref-type="corresp" rid="cor1">*</xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="I1">Center for Paralysis Research and Department of Anatomy, School of Veterinary Medicine, Purdue University, West Lafayette, IN 47907, USA</aff><author-notes><corresp id="cor1">*Christine B. Jaeger</corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>1994</year></pub-date><volume>5</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>223</fpage><lpage>232</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1994.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1994</copyright-year></permissions><abstract><p>Enteric neurons and glia cells were isolatedfrom adult Sprague Dawley rats. A procedure isdescribed using a combination of microdissectionand mechanical dissociation afterenzyme treatment which yields large numbers ofcell clusters suitable for tissue culture andgrafting into the injured spinal cord. Differentiatedenteric ganglia remained viable for atleast 5 days <italic>in vitro</italic> Cultured neurons expressedhistochemical reactivity for acetylcholinesteraseand nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphatediaphorase. Nestin positive glia, whichrepresented a population of non-myelinatingenteric Schwann cells, could also be identified incultures maintained 5 days or longer <italic>in vitro</italic>.The myenteric plexus of adult rats can provide areadily available source of neurons andSchwann cells for grafting to the central nervoussystem.</p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>